/* NICE in 5 Steps: a plain-English explainer under each step (5 per topic, in step order).
   Add five here whenever a topic is added to data.js. */
const EXPLAIN={
qrisk:[
 "Everyone gets advice on diet, activity, weight, smoking and alcohol. Before a statin, check a full lipid profile (non-fasting is fine) and ALT (a liver test), so you have a baseline to compare against.",
 "Primary prevention means the person has no heart disease or stroke yet. If their 10-year QRISK3 risk is 10% or more, offer atorvastatin 20 mg, a moderate dose.",
 "Established CVD means they have already had an MI, angina, stroke, TIA or PAD. This is secondary prevention: no QRISK3 is needed. Start high-intensity atorvastatin 80 mg straight away (lower if interactions, kidney disease or side effects).",
 "Non-HDL cholesterol is total cholesterol minus HDL. NICE's target is a fall of more than 40% from baseline. Recheck ALT at the same time: stop the statin only if it is more than 3 times the upper limit.",
 "First ask whether they actually take it. Then increase the statin dose. If they are on the most they can tolerate and still not at target, add ezetimibe."],
af:[
 "Haemodynamically unstable means the circulation is failing: very fast rate, low BP, fainting, chest pain or heart failure. This needs emergency treatment, often electrical cardioversion.",
 "If AF started less than 48 hours ago, the heart can be shocked back to normal rhythm (cardioversion) with a low risk of clot. After 48 hours, at least 3 weeks of anticoagulation is needed first, so timing matters.",
 "Rate control slows the heart rather than restoring rhythm. Use a beta-blocker (e.g. bisoprolol) or a rate-limiting calcium-channel blocker (diltiazem or verapamil). These calcium-channel blockers weaken a failing heart, so never use them in HFrEF (heart failure with reduced ejection fraction).",
 "CHA₂DS₂-VASc scores stroke risk: heart failure, hypertension, age, diabetes, previous stroke, vascular disease and female sex. Offer anticoagulation at 2 or more (consider at 1 in men). A DOAC (e.g. apixaban) is first line; check bleeding risk with ORBIT. On warfarin, an INR over 8 without bleeding means stop it and give oral vitamin K; INR 5–8 without bleeding means omit 1–2 doses.",
 "If symptoms continue despite rate control, refer to cardiology within 4 weeks. They can offer rhythm control: ablation, cardioversion or antiarrhythmic drugs."],
cardiomyopathy:[
 "A clue such as a murmur, an abnormal ECG or a family history, without warning signs, needs an echocardiogram to look at heart muscle thickness and pumping, plus a routine cardiology referral.",
 "Fainting during exercise can be the first sign of a life-threatening arrhythmia, especially in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM). Refer urgently and advise no strenuous exercise until a cardiologist has assessed them.",
 "In HCM the thick muscle can block blood leaving the heart. A beta-blocker slows the heart and eases this. Nitrates and other vasodilators lower the pressure and can make the blockage, and fainting, worse.",
 "Dilated cardiomyopathy is a large, weak heart, managed like heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF). If alcohol is the cause, stopping completely can let the heart recover.",
 "Many cardiomyopathies are inherited. Parents, siblings and children should be checked at an inherited cardiac conditions clinic with an ECG, echo and sometimes genetic testing."],
syncope:[
 "The 3 Ps are posture (standing or lying), provoking factors (pain, heat, standing) and prodrome (warning signs such as sweating or feeling sick). With lying and standing BP and an ECG, these sort out most causes.",
 "Red flags suggest a heart cause, which can be fatal: collapse during exercise, an abnormal ECG, a new murmur, heart failure, or a family history of sudden death under 40. These need specialist cardiovascular assessment within 24 hours.",
 "Features of a seizure include a bitten tongue (on the side), head turning, unusual posturing, prolonged jerking, and confusion afterwards. Refer to a first-fit or epilepsy clinic within 2 weeks, and give driving advice.",
 "Orthostatic hypotension is a fall in BP on standing (20 systolic or 10 diastolic). In older people it is often caused by medicines: alpha-blockers, diuretics, antihypertensives, nitrates or antidepressants. Review these first.",
 "A typical faint with a clear trigger and warning signs, and a normal ECG, needs only reassurance and advice. Simple faints standing up usually need no DVLA notification; other types follow the DVLA rules."],
pad:[
 "The ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) compares ankle BP with arm BP using a Doppler. 0.9 or less means narrowed arteries. Above 1.4 suggests stiff, calcified vessels (e.g. in diabetes), so the reading is unreliable.",
 "PAD counts as cardiovascular disease, so treat it like a heart attack for prevention. Stop smoking, and take clopidogrel 75 mg (antiplatelet) and atorvastatin 80 mg (high-intensity statin). Control BP and diabetes too.",
 "Intermittent claudication is calf pain on walking that settles with rest. Supervised exercise (walking until the pain is bad, about 2 hours a week for 3 months) builds new small blood vessels and is the most effective first treatment.",
 "If exercise hasn't helped enough, naftidrofuryl (a vasodilator) can be tried. Stop it if there's no benefit after 3–6 months. Alternatively, refer routinely to vascular surgeons for angioplasty or bypass.",
 "Critical limb ischaemia (pain at rest, often at night, ulcers or gangrene) means the limb is at risk: refer urgently. Acute ischaemia shows the 6 Ps: pain, pallor, pulseless, perishing cold, paraesthesia, paralysis. Call 999."],
vte:[
 "The Wells score combines clinical features into a probability of a clot. Two-level means 'likely' or 'unlikely'. PERC is 8 simple checks that can rule out PE without any tests, but only when you already think PE is unlikely.",
 "A likely DVT needs a leg ultrasound (proximal veins) within 4 hours. A likely PE needs a CT pulmonary angiogram (CTPA), arranged the same day through hospital referral.",
 "When a clot is unlikely, a D-dimer blood test (a clot breakdown product) is done first. Negative means no clot. Positive needs imaging, because D-dimer also rises with infection, pregnancy, cancer and age.",
 "If results will take longer than 4 hours, or a clot is confirmed, start a DOAC that can be continued (apixaban or rivaroxaban). In pregnancy use LMWH (low molecular weight heparin): DOACs and warfarin can harm the baby.",
 "A provoked clot has a temporary cause (surgery, plaster cast, immobility); once it has gone, 3 months is enough. An unprovoked clot has no clear cause and a higher chance of coming back, so consider continuing longer."],
rosacea:[
 "Rosacea is diagnosed by its look: flushing, central facial redness, sometimes spots, without blackheads. Avoid triggers (sun, heat, alcohol, spicy food) and use sunscreen daily. Topical steroids make rosacea worse.",
 "Papules and pustules are red bumps and pus spots. Topical ivermectin 1% once daily is first line; azelaic acid or metronidazole gels are alternatives.",
 "If spots are widespread or don't respond, add an oral tetracycline antibiotic (doxycycline or lymecycline). It works as an anti-inflammatory and is usually given for several weeks.",
 "Redness without spots doesn't respond to antibiotics. Brimonidine gel narrows the vessels for some hours. Make-up camouflage or laser treatment (privately or by referral) are other options.",
 "Ocular rosacea can affect the cornea (keratitis), which threatens sight: a painful red eye or light sensitivity needs urgent eye review. Rhinophyma (a thickened, bulbous nose) can be treated by a specialist."],
psoriasis:[
 "Check how much skin is involved, any high-impact sites (face, hands, feet, nails, genitals, scalp), and the effect on mood and life. PEST is a short questionnaire to spot psoriatic arthritis: use it every year.",
 "For plaques on the body and limbs, combine a potent steroid (e.g. betamethasone) and a vitamin D analogue (e.g. calcipotriol), each once a day at different times, for up to 4 weeks. Then use the vitamin D analogue alone if needed.",
 "Facial and flexural skin (groin, armpits, under the breasts) is thin and absorbs more steroid. Use only mild or moderate strength, for no more than 2 weeks at a time.",
 "BSA is body surface area: your palm is about 1%. More than 10%, uncontrolled disease or a big impact on life means referral for UV phototherapy or systemic drugs (methotrexate, ciclosporin, biologics).",
 "Pustular psoriasis (sheets of pus spots) and erythrodermic psoriasis (red over almost all the skin) are emergencies: the person can lose fluid and heat and get infections. A PEST score of 3 or more suggests arthritis: refer to rheumatology."],
lichen:[
 "Lichen simplex, lichen planus and lichen sclerosus are different conditions with similar names. Biopsy anything unusual, such as a lump, ulcer or thickened area that won't heal, to rule out skin cancer.",
 "Lichen simplex is thickened skin caused by repeated rubbing or scratching. It keeps going because the itch leads to more scratching. Covering the area, keeping nails short, emollients and a potent steroid break the cycle.",
 "Lichen planus has itchy purple flat-topped bumps and white lacy streaks in the mouth. Some medicines (e.g. ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers, NSAIDs) can trigger it. Painful erosive mouth disease goes to oral medicine.",
 "Lichen sclerosus, usually on the vulva, causes white, fragile, itchy skin. Clobetasol (a very potent steroid) daily for 4 weeks, then alternate days for 4 weeks, then twice weekly for 4 weeks, then as needed to keep it controlled.",
 "Lichen sclerosus has a small lifetime risk of squamous cell skin cancer. Review at least yearly for life. Any new lump, ulcer or area that won't heal needs an urgent biopsy."],
scabies:[
 "Scabies is diagnosed by the story and look: itch worse at night, burrows (short grey lines, often between fingers and on wrists), and other people at home itching too. No test is usually needed.",
 "Permethrin 5% cream goes over the whole body, including the scalp, face, ears and under the nails, and is washed off after 8–12 hours. A second application after 7 days kills mites that hatch from eggs.",
 "Everyone in the household and any close or sexual contacts are treated on the same day, even without itch. Otherwise the mites pass back and forth.",
 "Mites can live a few days away from skin. Washing clothes and bedding at 60°C, or sealing them in a bag for 3 days, kills them.",
 "The itch is an allergic reaction and can last 2–6 weeks after successful treatment. Only retreat if new burrows appear. Crusted (Norwegian) scabies is very contagious and needs specialist advice the same day."],
acne:[
 "Severity means the number and type of spots (blackheads, inflamed spots, nodules), plus scarring and pigmentation. Ask about mood: acne can seriously affect mental health. Advise non-oily products and not picking.",
 "For mild to moderate acne, offer a fixed combination gel for 12 weeks, such as adapalene with benzoyl peroxide (BPO). It treats blackheads and inflammation, and BPO reduces bacteria without causing antibiotic resistance.",
 "For moderate to severe acne, add an oral tetracycline (doxycycline or lymecycline) to the topical combination. Never use an oral antibiotic alone, because resistance develops.",
 "Judge the response at 12 weeks. Stop oral antibiotics by 6 months at most to limit resistance; continue a topical retinoid to keep acne away.",
 "Nodules, cysts or scarring mean referral to dermatology, usually for isotretinoin. Acne fulminans (sudden painful ulcerating nodules with fever and joint pain) needs same-day specialist advice."],
eczema:[
 "Emollients repair the skin barrier and reduce flares. Use plenty (adults up to 500 g a week) all over, every day, and as soap substitutes, even when the skin looks clear.",
 "Use the weakest steroid that works: mild (hydrocortisone) for the face and mild flares, moderate or potent for the body in worse flares. Apply once or twice daily until the flare settles, usually 1–2 weeks.",
 "If flares keep returning at the same sites, applying the steroid on 2 days a week (for example at weekends) prevents them. This is called weekend or proactive therapy.",
 "Eczema often looks crusted or weepy without needing antibiotics. If the person is well, don't routinely give antibiotics. Use oral flucloxacillin for widespread infection.",
 "Eczema herpeticum is herpes simplex spreading over eczema: painful punched-out sores, often with fever. It can be life-threatening. Start oral aciclovir and refer the same day. Eczema not controlled in primary care goes to dermatology."],
contact:[
 "Contact dermatitis comes from something touching the skin. Ask about work tasks, hand washing, gloves, cosmetics, jewellery (nickel) and hair dye, and whether it improves on holiday.",
 "Irritant dermatitis is caused by repeated exposure, e.g. water and soap. Avoid or reduce the cause, wash with a soap substitute, moisturise often, and wear suitable gloves (cotton liners under them).",
 "Steroid strength depends on where the rash is: mild on the face and eyelids (thin skin), potent on the hands (thick skin). Use short courses.",
 "Allergic contact dermatitis is an immune reaction to a specific substance. Patch testing in dermatology identifies it, so the person knows exactly what to avoid.",
 "If it's caused by work, refer and give a written diagnosis. RIDDOR is the health and safety reporting rule: the employer must report occupational dermatitis to the HSE."],
ie:[
 "Endocarditis is infection of the heart valves or lining. Suspect it when fever comes with a murmur, or in anyone with a prosthetic valve, previous endocarditis, or who injects drugs (IVDU).",
 "Refer the same day; call 999 if they show signs of sepsis. Don't start oral antibiotics in the community, because they can make blood cultures negative and hide the organism that needs targeted treatment.",
 "In hospital they take 3 sets of blood cultures from separate sites before antibiotics. TTE is a scan through the chest wall; TOE uses a probe in the oesophagus to see valves better. The Duke criteria combine these results to make the diagnosis.",
 "Treatment is weeks of IV antibiotics chosen from the cultures. Surgery is needed when the valve is destroyed causing heart failure, infection can't be controlled (such as an abscess), or large vegetations risk breaking off as emboli.",
 "High risk means a prosthetic valve (including TAVI) or previous endocarditis. Before an extraction or oral surgery, the dentist gives one dose of amoxicillin 2 g (or clarithromycin or azithromycin if allergic). Native valve disease is moderate risk: no antibiotics."],
pericarditis:[
 "Pericarditis is inflammation of the sac around the heart. The pain is sharp, worse lying flat and on breathing in, better leaning forward. The ECG shows ST elevation across many leads with a concave 'saddle' shape, and PR depression.",
 "ST elevation could be a heart attack, so in primary care send them for the same-day tests: troponin (heart muscle damage) and echo (fluid around the heart). Tamponade is fluid squeezing the heart (low BP, raised JVP, quiet heart sounds): call 999.",
 "Higher-risk features mean a serious cause or complication is more likely: fever over 38°C, onset over days to weeks, an effusion, raised troponin (myopericarditis), immunosuppression, or taking anticoagulants. These patients are admitted.",
 "First-line treatment is an anti-inflammatory (ibuprofen, or aspirin after an MI) with a PPI to protect the stomach, plus colchicine to cut recurrence. Colchicine is 500 micrograms once daily under 70 kg, twice daily at 70 kg or more.",
 "Exercise can bring back inflammation. Avoid strenuous activity until the pain has gone and CRP (an inflammation blood test) is normal. Review at about a week to check pain and CRP before tapering the anti-inflammatory."],
rheumatic:[
 "Rheumatic fever is an immune reaction after a group A strep throat infection. Jones criteria: major (carditis, migratory arthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum rash, skin nodules) and minor (fever, raised ESR or CRP, joint pain, long PR interval). You also need evidence of strep, such as a raised ASO titre.",
 "Children with suspected rheumatic fever need paediatric assessment the same day. Everyone has an echocardiogram, because heart inflammation can be present without a murmur.",
 "Penicillin V (phenoxymethylpenicillin) for 10 days clears any remaining strep. NSAIDs or aspirin treat the painful arthritis, which usually responds quickly.",
 "Regular penicillin (secondary prophylaxis) prevents new strep infections and further attacks, which cause most valve damage. Duration: no heart involvement, 5 years or until 21; heart involvement that healed, 10 years or until 21; lasting valve damage, 10 years or until 40. Whichever is longer.",
 "Years later the mitral valve can narrow (mitral stenosis), causing breathlessness and AF. With mitral stenosis and AF, use warfarin, because DOACs aren't proven here. Rheumatic valve disease is moderate risk for endocarditis, so no routine antibiotics before dental work."],
htn:["Clinic readings can be raised by anxiety (white-coat effect). Ambulatory BP monitoring (ABPM: a 24-hour cuff) or home monitoring (HBPM: twice daily for a week) confirms the diagnosis and the stage. Stage 1 is 135/85 or more on these; stage 2 is 150/95 or more.", "These tests look for damage to the kidneys, eyes and heart (target organ damage), and for clues to a secondary cause, such as low potassium (aldosteronism) or blood in the urine (kidney disease). QRISK3 gives the 10-year cardiovascular risk that helps decide on drug treatment.", "Severe hypertension is 180/120 or more. With bleeding or swelling at the back of the eye, or symptoms such as chest pain, confusion or kidney injury, refer the same day. Suspected phaeochromocytoma is also same day. Without these, repeat within 7 days, and start treatment straight away if organ damage is found.", "A = ACE inhibitor (or ARB, e.g. for people of Black African or Caribbean family origin), C = calcium-channel blocker (amlodipine), D = thiazide-like diuretic (indapamide). Diabetes overrides age: always start with A. If not controlled, add a second, then all three.", "Resistant hypertension is BP not controlled on A + C + D. Check adherence first. If potassium is 4.5 or less, add low-dose spironolactone; if higher, an alpha- or beta-blocker. Clinic targets are below 140/90 under 80, and below 150/90 at 80 or over."],
fh:["Familial hypercholesterolaemia (FH) is an inherited problem clearing LDL cholesterol, affecting about 1 in 250. Clues: very high cholesterol in an adult, tendon xanthomata (fatty lumps on the Achilles or hand tendons), and heart disease in relatives before 60.", "Confirm with at least two LDL measurements and exclude causes such as hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome and drugs. QRISK3 isn't used because people with FH are already high risk. Simon Broome and the Dutch Lipid Clinic Network score (DLCN) classify it; possible or definite FH, or DLCN over 5, means referral.", "Treatment is a high-intensity statin aiming for at least a 50% fall in LDL from baseline. Ezetimibe is added if needed. PCSK9 inhibitors (injections such as evolocumab) are started by specialists when LDL stays high.", "Because it's inherited, relatives are tested (cascade testing). Children of an affected parent are tested by age 10. Statins can harm a baby, so they're stopped 3 months before trying to conceive and immediately if pregnancy occurs.", "For primary prevention, start with what the person knows and fears, give risk as 'out of 100 people like you', and lay out options fairly using the NICE decision aid. A competent adult who declines after this has made a valid choice: record it and offer to revisit."],
aaa:["An AAA is an abdominal aorta 3.0 cm or wider. The NHS invites all men for a one-off ultrasound in the year they turn 65; older men who missed it can self-refer. Women aren't routinely screened.", "Small and medium aneurysms are watched with ultrasound rather than repaired, because early repair isn't safer at this size. Scans are yearly for 3.0–4.4 cm and every 3 months for 4.5–5.4 cm, with a vascular review within 12 weeks.", "At 5.5 cm the risk of rupture is high enough to consider repair, open surgery or EVAR (a stent graft through the groin). Fast growth (over 1 cm a year) also means referral within 2 weeks, whatever the size.", "Rupture causes sudden severe abdominal, back or loin pain with collapse or low BP. In an older man, especially a smoker, it's often mistaken for kidney stone pain. Call 999 and don't wait for imaging.", "Smoking speeds growth, so stopping matters most. Treat cardiovascular risk. Fluoroquinolone antibiotics (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) slightly raise the risk of aneurysm and dissection, so avoid them where possible."],
drive:["Group 1 is cars and motorcycles; Group 2 is lorries and buses (taxi drivers usually follow Group 2). The same event has different rules for each group, so this is the first thing to find in any question.", "After an ACS with a successful stent (PCI), car drivers stop for 1 week; without PCI, or after bypass surgery (CABG), 4 weeks. They don't need to tell the DVLA. Group 2 drivers must notify and stop for at least 6 weeks.", "Some events must be reported even for car drivers: a pacemaker (1 week off), an ICD for ventricular arrhythmia (6 months off), and unexplained blackouts (6 months off). Group 2 drivers with any ICD are barred permanently.", "The GMC steps: explain why they must stop and inform the DVLA, and offer a second opinion. Make every effort to persuade them. If they still drive, tell the DVLA medical adviser, letting the patient know first and confirming in writing.", "People self-certify for the first 7 days off. After that a fit note says either 'not fit' or 'may be fit for work' with suggestions (phased return, amended duties). Cardiac rehab improves recovery and return to work."],
ecg:["A fixed routine avoids missing things. Rate: 300 divided by the large squares between beats. Normal PR is 120–200 ms, QRS under 120 ms. QTc is the QT corrected for heart rate; over 500 ms is high risk.", "Two questions sort most rhythms. Irregularly irregular with no P waves is AF. Regular at about 150 with sawtooth waves is flutter. A P wave not followed by a QRS means heart block, and the PR pattern tells you which type.", "STEMI is ST elevation in two neighbouring leads; new LBBB (left bundle branch block) with chest pain is treated the same. VT is a regular broad-complex tachycardia. Complete heart block with symptoms needs pacing. All need 999.", "Mobitz II can suddenly become complete block. High potassium shows as peaked T waves and is dangerous once the ECG changes. Collapse with any ECG abnormality (bundle branch block, long QT, delta wave, Brugada) needs specialist review within 24 hours (NICE CG109).", "Many drugs prolong the QT (clarithromycin, citalopram, antipsychotics, methadone) or slow the heart (beta-blockers, verapamil, digoxin, donepezil). A normal ECG never excludes ACS, and can't exclude PE."],
brady:["A slow heart is normal in athletes and during sleep. Otherwise look for drugs (beta-blockers including eye drops, verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin, amiodarone, donepezil), high potassium, an underactive thyroid, or wear and tear of the conduction system.", "First-degree block: long PR, every beat conducted. Mobitz I (Wenckebach): PR lengthens until a beat drops, often benign. Mobitz II: beats drop suddenly with a fixed PR, which is dangerous. Complete block: atria and ventricles beat independently.", "Adverse features mean the slow rate is failing the circulation: low BP, fainting, heart failure or chest pain. In hospital, atropine is given and pacing arranged.", "Mobitz II, complete block and long pauses risk the heart stopping (asystole), so even a well patient goes the same day. Collapse with an abnormal ECG needs specialist cardiovascular review within 24 hours (NICE CG109).", "Stop or reduce drugs that slow the heart where you can, and never combine verapamil with a beta-blocker. Mobitz II and complete block need a pacemaker. Car drivers stop for 1 week after implantation and must tell the DVLA."],
tachy:["The story helps: SVT starts and stops suddenly and can be stopped by straining; flutter is regular at about 150; VT is broad and fast, often after heart damage; ectopics feel like a missed beat; sinus tachycardia builds gradually with a cause (fever, anaemia, anxiety).", "Palpitations with fainting, during exercise, with a family history of sudden death under 40, or with an abnormal ECG (pre-excitation, long QT, Brugada) may signal a dangerous rhythm and need urgent cardiology. Try to record the ECG during symptoms.", "Adverse features (low BP, fainting, heart failure, chest pain) need electrical cardioversion in hospital. A broad-complex tachycardia is treated as VT even if the patient looks well, and verapamil must never be given for it.", "The modified Valsalva is strain for 15 seconds, then lie flat straight away with the legs raised. If SVT continues, adenosine in hospital: 6 mg, then 12 mg, then 18 mg as rapid IV pushes. It can cause bronchospasm, so in asthma verapamil is used.", "Recurrent SVT is often cured by catheter ablation. Flutter carries the same stroke risk as AF, so anticoagulate by CHA₂DS₂-VASc. Avoid combining QT-prolonging drugs. Ectopics with a normal ECG and no red flags are benign: cut caffeine and reassure."],
acs:["Typical ACS is central chest pain or pressure over 15 minutes, spreading to the arm, jaw or back, with sweating and nausea. Older people, people with diabetes and women often have breathlessness, indigestion or collapse instead. A normal ECG never rules it out.", "Current pain, or recent pain with an abnormal ECG, means emergency admission. Give aspirin 300 mg to chew unless truly allergic, GTN if BP allows, and oxygen only if saturations are under 94%. Don't delay transfer to do an ECG.", "Troponin is a hospital test, so recently resolved pain still needs same-day hospital assessment. Pain more than 72 hours ago in a well patient can be assessed in primary care with an ECG, seeking cardiology advice if uncertain.", "Dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) is aspirin plus a second antiplatelet, usually ticagrelor, for 12 months, with aspirin continued for life. Add an ACE inhibitor, a beta-blocker and high-intensity atorvastatin, aiming for LDL 2.0 or less.", "Everyone should be offered cardiac rehabilitation. Car drivers stop for 1 week after a successful PCI (LVEF 40% or more), otherwise 4 weeks, without telling the DVLA. Watch for Dressler syndrome (pericarditis 2–6 weeks later) and depression."],
arrest:["Agonal gasps are slow, noisy, irregular breaths that happen during cardiac arrest. They are not normal breathing. Anyone unresponsive with absent or abnormal breathing needs 999 and CPR straight away; check a pulse for no more than 10 seconds.", "Push hard and fast in the centre of the chest, 5–6 cm deep, 100–120 a minute, letting the chest come back up. Give 2 breaths after every 30 compressions. Switch the AED on as soon as it arrives and follow its prompts.", "A shock doesn't restart the heart pumping immediately. Restart compressions straight away for 2 minutes before the next rhythm check. Pauses should be as short as possible.", "VF and pulseless VT are shockable. Adrenaline 1 mg and amiodarone 300 mg are given after the third shock (amiodarone 150 mg after the fifth). In PEA or asystole, give adrenaline as soon as possible. Adrenaline repeats every 3–5 minutes.", "The 4 Hs are hypoxia, hypovolaemia, hypo/hyperkalaemia and hypo/hyperthermia; the 4 Ts are thrombosis, tension pneumothorax, tamponade and toxins. A DNACPR decision is discussed with the patient, and all other treatment continues."],
hf:["Heart failure causes breathlessness (worse lying flat or at night), ankle swelling and tiredness. NT-proBNP is a blood test released when the heart is stretched; it decides how quickly someone needs an echo. An entirely normal ECG makes reduced EF unlikely.", "Over 2,000 ng/L means likely heart failure with a poorer outlook: echo and specialist within 2 weeks. 400–2,000: within 6 weeks. Under 400: less likely. Obesity and heart failure drugs lower the value; age over 70, kidney disease and AF raise it.", "Ejection fraction (EF) is the proportion of blood pumped out with each beat. HFrEF means 40% or less. NICE (2025) now says start all four drug groups, not one after another: an ACE inhibitor, a heart-failure beta-blocker, an MRA (spironolactone) and an SGLT2 inhibitor (dapagliflozin).", "ACE inhibitors and MRAs can raise potassium and creatinine, so check U&E before starting and 1–2 weeks after each change. NSAIDs and pioglitazone cause fluid retention; verapamil and diltiazem weaken the heart muscle.", "Weighing every morning catches fluid build-up early: a gain of 1.5–2 kg over 2–3 days means contacting the heart failure team. Offer cardiac rehab and flu and pneumococcal vaccines. Severe breathlessness at rest with low saturations is acute pulmonary oedema: 999."],
valve:["Aortic stenosis: ejection systolic murmur to the carotids, slow-rising pulse, soft second sound. Aortic regurgitation: early diastolic murmur, collapsing pulse. Mitral regurgitation: pansystolic to the axilla. Mitral stenosis: mid-diastolic rumble, often after rheumatic fever.", "Fainting on exertion with a systolic murmur suggests severe aortic stenosis, which can cause sudden death. NICE NG208 says refer for specialist assessment with echo within 2 weeks. Angina or breathlessness at rest with a murmur is also urgent.", "Many soft murmurs are innocent. An echo (heart ultrasound) is offered when the murmur comes with breathlessness, chest pain, swelling or an abnormal ECG, or when an ejection systolic murmur has a quiet second heart sound (a sign of aortic stenosis).", "Mild disease rarely causes problems. Moderate or severe disease needs specialist surveillance because timing of surgery matters. A bicuspid aortic valve needs follow-up even when mild, because the aorta can widen. Anyone with an artificial valve stays under cardiology.", "Mechanical valves need lifelong warfarin; DOACs cause clots and valve failure. AF with moderate–severe mitral stenosis also needs warfarin. Tissue valves and AF with other valve disease can usually use a DOAC."],
tia:["A stroke is a sudden loss of brain function lasting over 24 hours (or seen on a scan). A TIA has the same sudden symptoms but they fully resolve, usually within an hour. FAST is face, arm, speech, time. If symptoms are still present, treat it as a stroke and call 999: thrombolysis works only within 4.5 hours.", "Low blood sugar can cause one-sided weakness and confusion, so always check a capillary glucose. Migraine aura spreads gradually with positive symptoms (tingling, zigzags). Bell's palsy affects the whole side of the face including the forehead; a stroke spares the forehead. Isolated vertigo or fainting is not a TIA.", "Aspirin 300 mg given straight away reduces early stroke risk. NICE says refer immediately to be seen within 24 hours of onset. ABCD2 (a risk score) is no longer used to decide urgency, because it misses high-risk people. People on anticoagulants need imaging first, not aspirin.", "Dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) is aspirin plus clopidogrel for 21 days after a minor stroke or high-risk TIA, then clopidogrel alone for life. If the cause is AF, a DOAC replaces antiplatelets. Everyone gets a high-intensity statin and BP lowered to a clinic systolic below 130.", "A narrowed carotid artery (50–99% on the NASCET measure) that has caused symptoms benefits most from endarterectomy done early: refer within a week, surgery within 2 weeks. Car drivers stop for 1 month after a TIA or stroke; bus and lorry drivers for 1 year and must notify the DVLA."],
device:["A pacemaker paces a slow heart. An ICD (implantable cardioverter defibrillator) treats dangerous fast rhythms with rapid pacing or a shock. CRT (cardiac resynchronisation therapy) paces both ventricles in heart failure. The device card names the device and the centre that looks after it.", "Infection of the pocket or leads can spread to the heart (lead endocarditis) and usually means removing the whole system. Signs: redness, warmth, discharge, skin thinning over the box, or unexplained fever. Never aspirate or cut into the pocket, and take blood cultures in hospital before antibiotics.", "One shock with the person feeling well afterwards is usually an appropriate treatment of a fast rhythm: the device clinic checks it within a working day. Two or more shocks in 24 hours (an 'electrical storm' or device fault), or a shock with chest pain, breathlessness or blackout, needs 999.", "Pacemaker syndrome happens with ventricular-only pacing: the atria contract against closed valves, causing tiredness, dizziness and neck pulsation. Hiccups or rhythmic twitching suggest a lead stimulating the diaphragm. Both are fixed by reprogramming or repositioning.", "Car drivers stop for 1 week after a pacemaker (and notify), 6 months after an ICD shock or an ICD for VT. Near the end of life, discuss switching off ICD shocks: pacing continues, so it doesn't cause death. Devices must be removed before cremation because they can explode."],
angina:["The three features are: constricting discomfort in the chest, neck, jaw, shoulders or arms; brought on by exertion; relieved by rest or GTN within about 5 minutes. All three is typical angina, two is atypical, one or none is non-anginal. Rest pain or rapidly worsening angina is an acute coronary syndrome.", "NICE now offers CT coronary angiography (CTCA, a CT scan of the heart arteries) to everyone with typical or atypical angina, and to non-anginal pain with an abnormal ECG. If CTCA doesn't answer the question, a stress test with imaging follows. Exercise ECG is no longer used to diagnose angina.", "GTN (glyceryl trinitrate) spray relieves attacks and can be used before exertion. If pain continues 5 minutes after a second dose, call 999. Everyone also needs secondary prevention: aspirin 75 mg, atorvastatin 80 mg, an ACE inhibitor if diabetic, and risk factor control.", "Start a beta-blocker (e.g. bisoprolol) or a calcium-channel blocker. If one isn't tolerated, switch; if not controlled, combine. A beta-blocker must be combined with a dihydropyridine (amlodipine, MR nifedipine), never verapamil, because together they can stop the heart. Second-line drugs: long-acting nitrate, ivabradine, nicorandil, ranolazine.", "If angina isn't controlled on two anti-anginal drugs, refer to cardiology for PCI (a stent) or CABG (bypass surgery). A third drug is only used while waiting or if revascularisation isn't suitable."],
veins:["Varicose veins can bleed heavily from a minor knock, especially in older people on anticoagulants. Lie the person flat, raise the leg and press firmly. NICE says refer to a vascular service immediately.", "Referral is for veins causing symptoms (aching, heaviness, itching, swelling), skin damage from chronic venous insufficiency (brown pigmentation, eczema, hard tight skin called lipodermatosclerosis), venous leg ulcers (active or healed), and superficial vein thrombosis with suspected reflux. Asymptomatic cosmetic veins aren't referred.", "The vascular service uses duplex ultrasound to map the faulty veins. Endothermal ablation (heat sealing the vein from inside) is first choice, then ultrasound-guided foam sclerotherapy, then surgery. Compression hosiery relieves symptoms but doesn't treat the veins, so it's offered only when intervention isn't suitable (and in pregnancy).", "The ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) checks arterial supply before compression, because squeezing a leg with poor arteries can cause tissue death. 0.8–1.3: strong compression is safe. 0.5–0.8: arterial disease, refer. Under 0.5: urgent vascular, no compression. Over 1.3: calcified arteries, unreliable.", "Most ulcers carry bacteria without infection, so swab only if clinically infected (spreading redness, pain, heat, pus, fever) and then use flucloxacillin. An ulcer with a raised or rolled edge, or not healing after 12 weeks, may be a skin cancer and needs a biopsy."],
consent:["Consent is valid only if the person has capacity, has been given the information they need (including material risks: what a reasonable person in their position, and this person, would want to know, from the Montgomery judgment), and decides freely without pressure.", "Under the Mental Capacity Act everyone aged 16 or over is presumed to have capacity. Help them decide first (interpreters, aids, a better time). Making a decision others think is unwise is not, on its own, evidence of lacking capacity.", "Capacity is specific to one decision at one time. The person must be able to understand the information, retain it long enough, use or weigh it, and communicate a decision. If they can't, ask whether that's caused by an impairment or disturbance of the mind or brain (such as dementia or delirium).", "If an adult lacks capacity, a valid advance decision to refuse treatment (ADRT) is binding; for life-sustaining treatment it must be written, signed, witnessed and say 'even if my life is at risk'. Next is a health and welfare Lasting Power of Attorney (LPA). Otherwise the clinician decides in their best interests after consulting others, with an IMCA (independent advocate) if there is no one to consult.", "Under 16s can consent if they are Gillick competent (mature enough to understand fully). Contraception without parental involvement follows the Fraser guidelines. Sixteen and seventeen year olds are presumed to have capacity, but a refusal of life-saving treatment by anyone under 18 can be overridden by the court."],
rxsafe:["Prescriptions are legal documents. The patient's name and address, the prescriber's signature and the date are always needed, and the age must be written for children under 12. Abbreviations like 'mcg' and 'U' cause tenfold and hundredfold errors, so write micrograms and units in full.", "Schedule 2 and 3 drugs (strong opioids, stimulants, tramadol, buprenorphine, gabapentin, pregabalin, temazepam) need extra wording: the form, strength and an exact dose, plus the total quantity in both words and figures. Prescriptions for schedules 2, 3 and 4 expire 28 days after the date written.", "Good practice is no more than 30 days' supply of a controlled drug unless you can justify more. Schedule 2 and 3 drugs can't be put on batch repeat dispensing. Gabapentin and pregabalin moved to schedule 3 in 2019 because of misuse and deaths.", "The GMC says avoid prescribing for yourself or people close to you, and never controlled drugs except in a real emergency with no alternative. If a specialist asks you to prescribe, the responsibility is still yours. Unlicensed or off-label use needs a reason, evidence, a record and an informed patient.", "Report all suspected reactions to new black triangle medicines, and serious reactions to established ones, on a Yellow Card. Before starting opioids, gabapentinoids, benzodiazepines or z-drugs, agree with the patient how long they'll take them and how they'll stop (MHRA, 2026)."],
rxinteract:["Enzyme inducers such as carbamazepine, phenytoin, rifampicin and St John's wort speed up the breakdown of other drugs, lowering their levels: the pill can fail and the INR can fall. Inhibitors such as clarithromycin, fluconazole and amiodarone do the opposite, so levels rise and toxicity follows.", "On an enzyme inducer, oral, patch, ring and implant hormones may not work. Methods that aren't affected are the copper IUD, the hormonal IUS and the progestogen injection, and protection must continue for 28 days after the inducer stops. Ordinary antibiotics like amoxicillin don't affect the pill.", "Some pairs come up again and again: clarithromycin with simvastatin (myopathy: withhold the statin), trimethoprim with methotrexate (marrow suppression), an ACE inhibitor plus diuretic plus NSAID (acute kidney injury), and NSAIDs or ACE inhibitors with lithium (toxicity).", "Valproate can't be started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists agree there's no alternative, though men already taking it don't need this review. Topiramate now has a pregnancy prevention programme. Fluoroquinolones are reserved for when commonly recommended antibiotics aren't appropriate, because of disabling tendon, aortic and nerve effects.", "GLP-1 medicines shouldn't be used in pregnancy or while trying to conceive, so effective contraception is needed. Tirzepatide can reduce the effect of oral contraceptives, so add a barrier method or switch to a non-oral method for four weeks after starting and after each dose increase."],
rxmonitor:["Disease-modifying drugs like methotrexate can suppress the bone marrow and damage the liver, so blood counts, kidney and liver tests are checked every two weeks at first, then monthly for three months, then every three months. Azathioprine needs a TPMT test first, because people with low TPMT activity can't break it down.", "If neutrophils fall below 1.6, white cells below 3.5, platelets below 140, or ALT rises above 100, withhold the drug and talk to the specialist. A sore throat, fever, mouth ulcers or bruising could mean marrow suppression, so check an urgent blood count.", "Lithium has a narrow therapeutic range. Levels must be taken 12 hours after the last dose, every three months in the first year and every six months after that if stable. Kidney function, thyroid function and calcium are checked every six months because lithium can damage all three.", "After starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB, creatinine often rises a little. A rise under 30 percent is expected and you carry on. A rise of 30 percent or more means stop or reduce the drug and look for a cause. A potassium of 6.5 or more needs same-day hospital assessment.", "Antipsychotics cause weight gain, diabetes and high lipids, so check weight, blood pressure, HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks, at a year and then annually. Amiodarone affects the thyroid and liver, so check both every six months."],
rxsidefx:["ACE inhibitors cause a dry cough in about one in ten people by raising bradykinin; ARBs don't, so switch. Angioedema is a serious reaction: stop and never re-challenge. Amlodipine ankle swelling is from dilated arterioles, not fluid overload, so diuretics don't help.", "SGLT2 inhibitors can cause ketoacidosis with only modestly raised glucose, so check ketones in anyone unwell and stop the drug during acute illness. Long-term metformin lowers B12 absorption, causing neuropathy or anaemia.", "Oral bisphosphonates can inflame the oesophagus, so take them on waking with a full glass of water and stay upright for 30 minutes. Long-term use can cause unusual femur fractures (thigh or groin pain) and osteonecrosis of the jaw, so arrange dental checks first.", "Carbimazole can cause agranulocytosis: a sore throat, fever or mouth ulcers needs an urgent blood count. Amiodarone can cause thyroid disease, lung fibrosis, liver injury and photosensitivity.", "Finasteride and dutasteride roughly halve PSA, so double the value when interpreting. Tamoxifen raises the risk of womb cancer, so postmenopausal bleeding needs urgent referral. Oxybutynin is strongly anticholinergic and can cause confusion in frail older people."],
rxamr:["Most sore throats, ear infections, sinusitis and coughs get better without antibiotics. Explain how long they usually last, and offer a back-up prescription that the patient uses only if things don't improve or get worse.", "The FeverPAIN score (fever, pus, attending within three days, inflamed tonsils, no cough or cold) guides sore throat treatment: four or five points means phenoxymethylpenicillin may help. In otitis media, consider immediate amoxicillin if there's ear discharge, bilateral infection under two, or the child is unwell.", "For uncomplicated cystitis in women, three days of nitrofurantoin (if eGFR is 45 or more) or trimethoprim is enough. Men and pregnant women need seven days and a urine culture.", "In people over 65 or with catheters, bacteria in the urine are common and harmless, so dipsticks mislead: diagnose on symptoms. The exception is pregnancy, where asymptomatic bacteriuria is treated to prevent pyelonephritis.", "Flucloxacillin is first line for cellulitis; co-amoxiclav for bites and for cellulitis near the eyes or nose; hydrogen peroxide cream for localised impetigo. Ask what the 'penicillin allergy' was: diarrhoea is an intolerance, not an allergy."],
rxantidep:["An SSRI is usually the first antidepressant. Sertraline has the best safety evidence in heart disease. Citalopram and escitalopram prolong the QT interval, so their maximum doses are lower in people over 65.", "Review within two weeks of starting, or within one week for people aged 18 to 25 or at risk of suicide, because early agitation and suicidal thoughts can increase. Once well, continue for at least six months to prevent relapse.", "SSRIs reduce platelet serotonin, so with NSAIDs, aspirin or anticoagulants they raise bleeding risk: add a PPI or choose differently. Combining with other serotonergic drugs like tramadol, triptans or MAOIs can cause serotonin syndrome.", "Serotonin syndrome starts within hours of a new serotonergic drug or dose increase, with clonus, brisk reflexes, sweating, fever and diarrhoea. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome comes on over days with antipsychotics, with rigid muscles, slow reflexes and a very high CK.", "Stopping suddenly causes discontinuation symptoms such as dizziness and electric-shock sensations, especially with paroxetine and venlafaxine. Reduce gradually. Switching to an MAOI needs a washout: five weeks after fluoxetine because of its long half-life."],
rxrenal:["Many drugs are cleared by the kidneys. Metformin needs review when eGFR is under 45 and stopping under 30. Nitrofurantoin doesn't work well and is more toxic under 45. Gabapentin, pregabalin, lithium, digoxin and DOACs need lower doses.", "eGFR overestimates kidney function in older, thin people. For DOACs and drugs with a narrow therapeutic range, calculate creatinine clearance with the Cockcroft–Gault formula, especially at age 75 or over or at extremes of weight.", "During vomiting, diarrhoea or fever, pause sulfonylureas, ACE inhibitors, diuretics, metformin, ARBs, NSAIDs and SGLT2 inhibitors (SADMANS), and restart them 24 to 48 hours after eating and drinking normally.", "Acute kidney injury is a creatinine rise of 26 micromol/L within 48 hours, or one and a half times baseline within a week. In liver disease, sedatives and opioids can trigger encephalopathy and NSAIDs cause bleeding and fluid retention; usual-dose paracetamol is generally safe.", "Older people are more sensitive to sedatives, anticholinergics and blood pressure drugs, which cause falls and confusion. STOPP flags drugs like glibenclamide, long-term benzodiazepines and oxybutynin in frailty; aspirin for primary prevention should be stopped."],
rxpreg:["Everyone trying to conceive should take folic acid 400 micrograms until 12 weeks. Take 5 mg if on antiepileptic drugs, with diabetes, a BMI of 30 or more, or a previous neural tube defect.", "ACE inhibitors and ARBs damage fetal kidneys, so stop within two days of a positive test and switch, usually to labetalol. Warfarin causes embryopathy from six weeks, so switch to heparin. Valproate needs an urgent specialist plan rather than a sudden stop.", "Thyroxine requirements rise early in pregnancy, so increase levothyroxine by 25 to 50 micrograms when pregnancy is confirmed. For nausea, antihistamines like cyclizine come first; ondansetron is second line, with a very small cleft risk in the first trimester.", "Avoid trimethoprim in the first trimester (folate antagonist), tetracyclines (teeth and bone), nitrofurantoin at term, NSAIDs, and live vaccines like MMR. Pertussis vaccine is given from 16 weeks and RSV vaccine from 28 weeks.", "Codeine can make babies dangerously drowsy if the mother is an ultra-rapid metaboliser, so avoid it while breastfeeding. Ibuprofen, paracetamol, sertraline and enalapril are compatible. The combined pill is UKMEC 4 under six weeks after birth while breastfeeding."],
rxcalc:["A percentage strength is grams per 100 mL, so 1 percent is 10 mg per mL. A ratio like 1 in 1,000 is 1 gram in 1,000 mL, which is 1 mg per mL. To find a volume, divide the dose you need by the concentration.", "Adrenaline for anaphylaxis is given into the thigh muscle using the 1 in 1,000 strength: 500 micrograms (0.5 mL) for adults and children over 12, 300 micrograms for 6 to 12 year olds, and 150 micrograms from six months to six years. Repeat after five minutes if needed.", "Codeine and tramadol are about a tenth as strong as oral morphine. Subcutaneous morphine is about twice as strong as oral, and subcutaneous diamorphine about three times, so divide the oral morphine dose by two or three.", "A breakthrough dose is a tenth to a sixth of the total daily opioid dose. For steroids, prednisolone 5 mg equals hydrocortisone 20 mg and dexamethasone 750 micrograms.", "Alcohol units are the percentage strength times the volume in mL, divided by 1,000. Pack-years are packs (20 cigarettes) a day times years smoked. Creatinine clearance uses age, weight, sex and serum creatinine."],
rxdeprescribe:["A structured medication review starts with what matters to the patient, identifies essential drugs, stops unnecessary ones, then checks effectiveness, safety, cost and whether they can and will take them. After hospital discharge, reconcile the medicines list within a week.", "Aspirin for primary prevention causes more bleeding than it prevents. PPIs without an ongoing reason can be stepped down to on-demand use. Antipsychotics in dementia increase stroke and death, so review at six weeks and aim to stop.", "After five years of an oral bisphosphonate (three of zoledronate), reassess fracture risk. If it's low, a drug holiday is reasonable, with review; if high, continue.", "Benzodiazepines are reduced slowly, often switching to diazepam and cutting about an eighth every two weeks. Steroids taken for more than three weeks need tapering to avoid adrenal crisis. Beta-blockers stopped suddenly can cause rebound angina.", "Between a third and a half of medicines for long-term conditions aren't taken as intended. Ask without judgement. Intentional non-adherence comes from beliefs and concerns, which need discussing; a dosette box only helps practical problems like forgetting."],
calc22:["Put the test result in the rows and the true disease status (the reference standard) in the columns. a is a true positive, b a false positive, c a false negative and d a true negative. Labelling the cells first stops almost every error.", "Sensitivity starts from everyone who has the disease: how many did the test pick up? Specificity starts from everyone without it: how many did the test correctly clear? Both work down the columns, and both belong to the test itself.", "Predictive values start from the result, which is what the patient knows. Of everyone who tested positive, how many really have the disease (PPV)? Of everyone who tested negative, how many are truly clear (NPV)? They work across the rows.", "When a question gives only prevalence, sensitivity and specificity, build a table for 1,000 people. Split them by prevalence, apply sensitivity to the diseased column and specificity to the healthy column, then read off the cell you need.", "In a population where the disease is more common, a positive result is more likely to be true, so PPV rises and NPV falls. Sensitivity and specificity don't change. A very sensitive test rules out when negative (SnNout); a very specific test rules in when positive (SpPin)."],
calclr:["A positive likelihood ratio says how many times more likely a positive result is in someone with the disease than in someone without: sensitivity divided by (1 minus specificity). The negative LR is (1 minus sensitivity) divided by specificity.", "Likelihood ratios work on odds, not probabilities. Odds are the chance it happens divided by the chance it doesn't: 20% is 0.2 ÷ 0.8 = 0.25. To go back, divide the odds by 1 plus the odds: odds of 3 is 75%.", "Start with the pre-test probability (prevalence, a clinical score or your estimate), convert it to odds, multiply by the LR of the result, and convert back. A 50% pre-test probability and an LR of 4 gives odds of 4, so 80%.", "An LR over 10 causes a large rise in probability and often rules a diagnosis in; under 0.1 a large fall that often rules it out. Values around 2 or 0.5 shift it by only about 15 percentage points, and an LR of 1 changes nothing.", "Because LRs are built from sensitivity and specificity, they don't change with prevalence. The commonest exam error is multiplying the probability by the LR: 10% × 9 is not 90%, it's odds of 1, or 50%."],
calcrisk:["Every trial result starts with two event rates: the control event rate (CER, the outcome in the placebo or usual-care group) and the experimental event rate (EER, the outcome in the treated group).", "The absolute risk reduction is the simple difference, CER minus EER. The relative risk reduction is that difference as a proportion of the control rate. The relative risk is EER divided by CER, so RRR equals 1 minus RR.", "The number needed to treat is 1 divided by the ARR (as a decimal), rounded up, and always for the trial's time period: 'treat 20 people for 5 years to prevent one stroke'. The number needed to harm uses the absolute risk increase of a side effect.", "Case-control studies start from people with and without the outcome, so risks can't be calculated; they report an odds ratio, ad ÷ bc. When the outcome is rare, the odds ratio is close to the relative risk.", "A ratio (RR, OR, HR) whose 95% confidence interval includes 1 isn't statistically significant; for a difference (ARR) the no-effect value is 0. NICE NG197 asks us to explain risk with absolute numbers and natural frequencies, not relative risks alone."],
calcdist:["The mean adds the values and divides by how many there are. The median is the middle value once they're in order (average the middle two for an even number). The mode is the most frequent. For skewed data or outliers, the median and interquartile range describe the data better.", "Skew is named after the tail. With a long tail of high values (length of stay, costs), the mean is pulled up past the median: mean > median > mode is positive skew. The reverse is negative skew. In a normal distribution all three are equal.", "In a normal distribution, 68% of values lie within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2 (strictly 1.96) and 99.7% within 3. Reference ranges are mean ± 2 SD, so 1 in 20 healthy people is 'abnormal'. A z-score is how many SDs a value sits from the mean.", "The standard deviation describes how spread out individuals are; the standard error describes how precise the sample mean is. SE = SD ÷ √n, and the 95% confidence interval for the mean is mean ± 1.96 × SE. Bigger samples give narrower intervals.", "Incidence counts new cases over a period; prevalence counts everyone with the condition at a point. Prevalence is roughly incidence × average duration, so longer survival raises prevalence. Percentage change always divides by the old value."],
calcdose:["A percentage strength means grams in 100 mL, so 1% is 10 mg/mL and lidocaine 2% is 20 mg/mL. A ratio means grams in that many mL: 1 in 1,000 adrenaline is 1 mg/mL and 1 in 10,000 is 0.1 mg/mL (100 micrograms/mL).", "The volume to give is the dose divided by the concentration. For weight-based doses, multiply the mg/kg by the weight first, then check against the maximum (a child's dose shouldn't exceed an adult's). A 16 kg child at 15 mg/kg needs 240 mg: 10 mL of 120 mg/5 mL.", "The quantity to supply is the number of tablets (or mL) per day times the number of days: two tablets twice daily for 28 days is 112. Insulin is 100 units/mL, so 0.2 mL is 20 units. Fluids: 1 litre over 8 hours is 125 mL/hour; drops a minute use 20 drops/mL.", "IM adrenaline 1 in 1,000 for anaphylaxis is 500 micrograms over 12, 300 micrograms aged 6 to 12, and 150 micrograms from 6 months to 6 years. Benzylpenicillin for suspected meningococcal disease is 1.2 g from 10, 600 mg aged 1 to 9, and 300 mg under 1. Buccal midazolam is 10, 7.5, 5 or 2.5 mg by age.", "Most dose errors are tenfold: write 0.5 mg (never .5 or 5.0 mg), write micrograms and units in full, keep units the same before dividing, and estimate the answer before you calculate it."],
calcopioid:["Convert the patient's total 24-hour opioid dose into oral morphine first, then into whatever you need. Working with single doses is the commonest error.", "Codeine, dihydrocodeine and tramadol are roughly one-tenth as strong as oral morphine. Subcutaneous morphine is twice as strong as oral (divide by 2), and subcutaneous diamorphine three times as strong (divide by 3). Oral oxycodone is about 1.5 to 2 times as strong as oral morphine.", "A buprenorphine 5 micrograms/hour patch is roughly 12 mg of oral morphine a day; a fentanyl 12 micrograms/hour patch about 30 mg (25 ≈ 60 mg). Patches take 12 to 24 hours to work and to wear off, so they suit stable pain.", "The breakthrough dose is one-tenth to one-sixth of the total 24-hour dose, by the same route: MR 45 mg twice daily (90 mg a day) means 15 mg immediate-release. NICE CG140 starts opioid-naive adults on 20 to 30 mg a day with 5 mg for breakthrough, and a laxative for everyone.", "Prednisolone 5 mg equals hydrocortisone 20 mg, methylprednisolone 4 mg and dexamethasone 750 micrograms. Anyone on prednisolone 5 mg or more for 4 weeks or longer needs a Steroid Emergency Card; when ill with fever, they double the dose, and need IM hydrocortisone if vomiting."],
calckidney:["Cockcroft–Gault estimates creatinine clearance: (140 minus age) times weight in kg, times 1.23 for men or 1.04 for women, divided by serum creatinine in µmol/L. A 70-year-old woman of 55 kg with creatinine 110 has about 36 mL/min.", "The BNF and DOAC licences use creatinine clearance, which matters most in older, lighter people where eGFR can look falsely reassuring. In obesity, use ideal body weight: 50 kg for men or 45.5 kg for women, plus 2.3 kg per inch over 5 feet.", "CKD is staged by eGFR (G1 90+, G2 60–89, G3a 45–59, G3b 30–44, G4 15–29, G5 under 15) and by urine albumin:creatinine ratio (A1 under 3, A2 3–30, A3 over 30 mg/mmol), confirmed over 3 months. G1 and G2 are only CKD with another marker.", "AKI is a creatinine rise of 26 µmol/L or more within 48 hours, or to 1.5 times baseline within 7 days, or low urine output. Stage 1 is 1.5 to 1.9 times baseline, stage 2 is 2 to 2.9 times, stage 3 is 3 times or more (or 354 µmol/L or more).", "After starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB, a creatinine rise under 30% is expected: continue and recheck. A rise of 30% or more, or potassium of 6.0 or more, means stopping or reducing. Refer for an ACR of 70 or more, ACR 30+ with haematuria, or a 5-year kidney failure risk over 5%."],
calccv:["NICE NG136: clinic 140/90 or more with ABPM or home average 135/85 or more is stage 1; clinic 160/100 with ABPM or home 150/95 is stage 2. Clinic 180 systolic or 120 diastolic is severe hypertension, needing same-day assessment for target organ damage.", "For home monitoring, take two readings a minute apart, twice a day, for at least 4 (ideally 7) days. Discard day 1 and average the rest. Treatment targets are under 140/90 in clinic below age 80, and under 150/90 from 80.", "CHA₂DS₂-VASc scores heart failure, hypertension, age 75+ (2), diabetes, stroke or TIA (2), vascular disease, age 65–74 and female sex. Offer anticoagulation at 2 or more; consider it for men scoring 1. ORBIT (age 75+, low Hb 2, bleeding 2, eGFR under 60, antiplatelets) flags modifiable bleeding risks.", "ABPI is the highest ankle pressure divided by the highest arm pressure: 0.9 to 1.3 is normal, under 0.8 rules out full compression, under 0.5 needs urgent referral, and over 1.3 suggests calcified arteries. Orthostatic hypotension is a fall of 20 systolic or 10 diastolic within 3 minutes of standing.", "On a statin, aim for a fall of more than 40% in non-HDL cholesterol (total minus HDL) at 3 months. On an ECG at 25 mm/s, heart rate is 300 divided by the large squares between R waves, and QTc (Bazett) is QT divided by the square root of the RR interval."],
calclife:["Alcohol units equal the strength (ABV %) times the volume in mL, divided by 1,000. A pint is 568 mL, so a pint of 4% beer is 2.3 units and a 750 mL bottle of 12% wine is 9. The UK Chief Medical Officers advise no more than 14 units a week, spread over 3 or more days.", "AUDIT-C (three questions) screens: 5 or more means completing the full AUDIT. On the full AUDIT, 0–7 is lower risk, 8–15 increasing risk (brief intervention), 16–19 higher risk, and 20 or more possible dependence (assess withdrawal risk and refer).", "Pack-years measure lifetime smoking: cigarettes a day divided by 20 (a pack), times years smoked. Twenty a day for 30 years is 30 pack-years; 30 a day for 25 years is 37.5.", "BMI is weight in kg divided by height in metres squared. Overweight is 25–29.9 and obesity 30 or more, but for people of South Asian, Chinese, other Asian, Middle Eastern, Black African or African-Caribbean background, overweight starts at 23 and obesity at 27.5.", "For adults with a BMI under 35, waist-to-height ratio adds central adiposity: 0.4–0.49 healthy, 0.5–0.59 increased risk, 0.6 or more high risk. Adults should aim for 150 minutes of moderate (or 75 vigorous) activity a week plus strength work on 2 days."],
calcscores:["The two-level Wells DVT score gives a point for each clinical feature and subtracts 2 if another diagnosis is as likely: 2 or more is DVT likely (ultrasound within 4 hours), 1 or less unlikely (D-dimer). For PE, signs of DVT and 'PE most likely' score 3 each; over 4 is likely (CTPA).", "CRB65 scores confusion, respiratory rate 30+, systolic under 90 or diastolic 60 or less, and age 65+: 0 treat at home, 1–2 consider hospital, 3–4 urgent admission. For sore throats, FeverPAIN 4–5 or Centor 3–4 suggests an immediate or back-up antibiotic.", "PHQ-9 bands are 5 mild, 10 moderate, 15 moderately severe and 20 severe; NICE NG222 calls under 16 'less severe' and 16 or more 'more severe'. Any score on item 9 needs a same-day risk assessment. GAD-7 bands are 5, 10 and 15; 10 or more suggests GAD.", "QRISK3 estimates 10-year cardiovascular risk for ages 25 to 84. At 10% or more, offer atorvastatin 20 mg after lifestyle advice. It isn't used in type 1 diabetes, CKD with eGFR under 60 or albuminuria, or familial hypercholesterolaemia, where people are already high risk.", "FRAX (40–90, can include bone density) and QFracture (30–99, can't) estimate 10-year fracture risk. NICE NG259 (2026): consider DXA when the major osteoporotic fracture risk is 10% or more, and go straight to DXA after a hip or vertebral fragility fracture. A T-score of −2.5 or below is osteoporosis."],
calcdates:["Naegele's rule estimates the due date from the first day of the last period: add a year, take away 3 months and add 7 days (add the extra days if the cycle is longer than 28). Gestation is the number of days since the LMP divided by 7, written as weeks + days.", "Levonorgestrel works up to 72 hours after unprotected sex; ulipristal up to 120 hours (then wait 5 days before starting hormonal contraception); the copper IUD up to 120 hours, or 5 days after the earliest likely ovulation, and is the most effective.", "Quick-starting outside days 1 to 5 needs extra precautions: 7 days for combined methods (9 for Qlaira), 2 days for the progestogen-only pill, 7 days for the implant, injection and IUS, none for a copper IUD. Desogestrel is late after 12 hours, a traditional POP after 3.", "From July 2026 the schedule is: 8 weeks 6-in-1, MenB, rotavirus; 12 weeks 6-in-1, MenB, rotavirus; 16 weeks 6-in-1, PCV; 1 year MMRV, PCV, MenB; 18 months 6-in-1 and MMRV; 3 years 4 months the preschool booster. In England, HPV-negative cervical screening is 5-yearly from 25 to 64.", "Car drivers (group 1) stop for 1 week after successful PCI or a pacemaker, 4 weeks after an MI without PCI or CABG, 1 month after a TIA or stroke, 6 months after a first unprovoked seizure and 12 months seizure-free with epilepsy."]
};
